    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, i'm looking at a contract for a shipment of electronic components coming from South Korea to Germany. It says CIF. can you explain exactly what the seller is responsible for under CIF Incoterms 2020?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Under CIF Incoterms 2020, the seller is responsible for delivering the goods to the named port of destination in Germany, but they do not take on the risk of loss or damage once the goods are loaded onto the vessel at the port of shipment in South Korea. Instead, the seller must arrange and pay for the main carriage and insurance to the destination port, while the buyer bears all risks and costs from that point onward.\n\nIt's a bit like a contract where the seller hands over the goods to a \"goblin courier\" who is tasked with delivering them all the way to the buyer's doorstep, but the buyer still has to worry if the goblin courier gets lost or if the goods get stolen along the way. The seller only needs to ensure the goblin courier has a valid ticket and a little insurance policy to cover the journey.\n\nSo, while the seller pays for the freight and insurance, the risk of the goods being damaged or lost during transit remains with the buyer. This is a classic case of a seller who is happy to pay the goblin courier's fees but doesn't want to be held responsible if the goblin courier decides to drop the package in a swamp or get eaten by a dragon.",
